(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-12-01"] = {"phy-12-01-m01":{"idea":"Moment of inertia adds up mass times distance squared. A thin ring has all its mass at the same distance R.","steps":["I = Σ m r².","Every bit of the ring is at distance R from the axis.","So I = (Σ m) R² = MR²."],"others":"2MR²/5 is a solid sphere. MR²/4 and MR²/2 are not ring values; MR²/2 belongs to a disc.","trap":"Ring about its centre axis: MR².","answer":"MR²"},"phy-12-01-m02":{"idea":"A disc has its mass spread inwards, so its I is smaller than a ring's.","steps":["Add up thin rings from the centre to the edge.","This gives I = ½ MR².","That is half of the ring value for the same mass and radius."],"others":"2MR²/5 is a solid sphere. MR² is a ring. MR²/4 is not a standard value for a disc about this axis.","trap":"Disc: ½MR². Ring: MR².","answer":"MR²/2"},"phy-12-01-m03":{"idea":"A solid sphere about a diameter has I = (2/5)MR².","steps":["Add the contributions of thin discs across the sphere.","The result is I = (2/5)MR².","So the answer is 2MR²/5."],"others":"7MR²/5 is for a solid sphere about a tangent. MR²/2 is a disc. 2MR²/3 is a thin hollow sphere.","trap":"Do not mix the solid sphere (2/5) with the hollow sphere (2/3).","answer":"2MR²/5"},"phy-12-01-m04":{"idea":"A thin hollow sphere has all its mass at the full radius, so its I is bigger than a solid sphere's.","steps":["All mass is on the shell at distance R.","Averaging over directions about a diameter gives I = (2/3)MR².","This is larger than (2/5)MR² for a solid sphere."],"others":"MR²/2 is a disc. MR² is a ring. 2MR²/5 is a solid sphere.","trap":"Hollow: 2/3. Solid: 2/5.","answer":"2MR²/3"},"phy-12-01-m05":{"idea":"A rod's moment of inertia depends on the axis. About its centre it is smallest.","steps":["For a thin rod about the axis through its centre, perpendicular to it: I = ML²/12.","About one end it would be ML²/3.","The question says the centre."],"others":"ML²/3 is for the end. ML²/2 and ML²/6 are not standard rod values.","trap":"Centre: 1/12. End: 1/3.","answer":"ML²/12"},"phy-12-01-m06":{"idea":"The parallel axis theorem shifts the axis away from the centre of mass.","steps":["The new moment of inertia is I = I_cm + M d².","The extra term Md² is always positive.","So the centre-of-mass axis gives the smallest I of all parallel axes."],"others":"Minus Md² would make I smaller than I_cm, which is impossible. Md has the wrong units. ½Md² is not the theorem.","trap":"It is d squared, and it is added.","answer":"I = I_cm + Md²"},"phy-12-01-m07":{"idea":"The perpendicular axis theorem works only for flat bodies.","steps":["The body must be a plane (laminar) sheet.","The x and y axes lie in the plane and meet at a point.","The z axis is perpendicular to the plane through that point, and I_z = I_x + I_y."],"others":"It does not depend on constant rotation. It does not apply to any solid 3D body, and it is not limited to a solid sphere.","trap":"Flat body only.","answer":"a flat (laminar) body, with x and y axes in its plane"},"phy-12-01-m08":{"idea":"Moment of inertia depends on where the mass is, relative to the axis.","steps":["I = Σ m r².","It depends on the masses and their distances from the axis.","It does not depend on how fast the body turns or on the torque."],"others":"Angular velocity, torque and angular momentum describe the motion, not the shape of the body.","trap":"Change the axis and the moment of inertia changes.","answer":"the distribution of its mass and the position of the axis"},"phy-12-01-m09":{"idea":"Radius of gyration k is defined by I = Mk².","steps":["For a solid sphere I = (2/5)MR².","Mk² = (2/5)MR², so k² = (2/5)R².","k = R√(2/5) ≈ 0.63 R."],"others":"R√(2/3) is for a hollow sphere. R√(3/5) and R/√2 do not match any common case.","trap":"k is the square root of I/M.","answer":"R√(2/5)"},"phy-12-01-m10":{"idea":"Use I = Mk² with the hollow sphere value.","steps":["Mk² = (2/3)MR².","k² = (2/3)R².","k = R√(2/3) ≈ 0.82 R."],"others":"R√(2/5) is the solid sphere. R/√3 and R√(3/2) come from wrong fractions.","trap":"Take the square root of 2/3, not 2/3 itself.","answer":"R√(2/3)"},"phy-12-01-m11":{"idea":"Torque is force times perpendicular distance, so its unit is the newton metre.","steps":["τ = r × F.","The unit is metre × newton = N m.","It is not the same as the joule, even though the units look alike."],"others":"kg m/s is momentum. The watt is power. The newton second is impulse.","trap":"N m for torque, J for energy: different quantities.","answer":"newton metre (N m)"},"phy-12-01-m12":{"idea":"Angular momentum is the rotational partner of ordinary momentum.","steps":["Linear momentum is p = mv.","The rotational version swaps m for I and v for ω.","So L = Iω."],"others":"I/ω is not a physical quantity here. ½Iω² is the rotational kinetic energy. Iω² has no meaning on its own.","trap":"L = Iω, KE = ½Iω².","answer":"L = Iω"},"phy-12-01-m13":{"idea":"Newton's second law for rotation is τ = Iα.","steps":["Linear law: F = ma.","Replace F with torque τ, m with I, a with α.","So τ = Iα."],"others":"I/α and α/I are inverted. Iα² has the wrong form.","trap":"Think F = ma and translate each symbol.","answer":"τ = Iα"},"phy-12-01-m14":{"idea":"Torque is the rate of change of angular momentum.","steps":["τ = dL/dt.","The net external torque causes L to change.","If the torque is zero, L stays constant."],"others":"Kinetic energy, moment of inertia and angular velocity are not equal to dL/dt.","trap":"Compare with F = dp/dt.","answer":"the net external torque on it"},"phy-12-01-m15":{"idea":"Angular momentum is conserved when no external torque acts.","steps":["τ = dL/dt.","If τ = 0, then L does not change.","Kinetic energy, I and ω can still change while L stays the same."],"others":"Constant kinetic energy, constant I or constant ω are not the condition.","trap":"The condition is zero net external torque.","answer":"the net external torque on it is zero"},"phy-12-01-m16":{"idea":"With no external torque, a smaller moment of inertia means a faster spin.","steps":["No external torque acts, so L = Iω is constant.","Pulling the arms in makes I smaller.","So ω must get larger to keep Iω the same."],"others":"ω does not decrease when I decreases. L does not increase, because it is conserved. ω does not stay the same, since I has changed.","trap":"Kinetic energy rises too, because her muscles do work.","answer":"increases, because her moment of inertia decreases and L is constant"},"phy-12-01-m17":{"idea":"Adding mass at the rim raises I, so the disc slows down while L stays fixed.","steps":["No external torque about the axis, so Iω is constant.","The clay sticks at the rim and adds to I.","So ω must fall."],"others":"It does not stop at once. The clay has no spin, but it still adds to I. Extra mass does not speed the disc up.","trap":"Think of an ice skater spreading arms.","answer":"decreases, because L is conserved while I increases"},"phy-12-01-m18":{"idea":"Two identical discs joined together double the moment of inertia, so the angular speed halves.","steps":["Before: only one disc spins, so L = Iω.","After: both spin together, so L = (I + I)ω'.","Conserving L: Iω = 2Iω', so ω' = ω/2."],"others":"ω/4 would lose too much. ω would need no change in I. 2ω would increase the speed, which is impossible here.","trap":"Kinetic energy is not conserved: half is lost as heat.","answer":"ω/2"},"phy-12-01-m19":{"idea":"For uniform circular motion about the centre, angular momentum does not change.","steps":["L = m v r.","The speed and the radius are constant, so L has constant size.","The centripetal force passes through the centre, so there is no torque, and the direction of L (along the axis) is fixed."],"others":"It is not zero, because v and r are non-zero. It does not change in direction or size.","trap":"The direction of L is along the axis, not along the velocity.","answer":"constant in magnitude and in direction"},"phy-12-01-m20":{"idea":"Only the part of the force at right angles to the spanner makes torque.","steps":["τ = r F sin θ.","r = 2 m, F = 10 N, θ = 30°.","τ = 2 × 10 × 0.5 = 10 N m."],"others":"20 N m would be for a force at 90°. 17.32 uses cos 30°. 5 N m uses half of r.","trap":"Use sin θ when θ is the angle between the force and the spanner.","answer":"10 N m"},"phy-12-01-m21":{"idea":"Rotational kinetic energy is the partner of ½mv².","steps":["Linear: ½ m v².","Replace m with I and v with ω.","So KE = ½ I ω²."],"others":"Iω is angular momentum. ½Iω and Iω² have the wrong form.","trap":"Square the ω, and keep the half.","answer":"½Iω²"},"phy-12-01-m22":{"idea":"Rotational power is the partner of P = Fv.","steps":["Work done by a torque: dW = τ dθ.","Divide by time: P = τ (dθ/dt).","So P = τ ω."],"others":"τ/ω and ω/τ do not give watts. τω² has an extra ω.","trap":"Linear: Fv. Rotational: τω.","answer":"τω"},"phy-12-01-m23":{"idea":"For a rolling sphere, part of the energy is in spinning and part in moving forward.","steps":["For a sphere I = (2/5)MR² and ω = v/R.","Rotational KE = ½ × (2/5)MR² × (v/R)² = (1/5)Mv².","Total = ½Mv² + (1/5)Mv² = (7/10)Mv².","Fraction = (1/5)/(7/10) = 2/7."],"others":"5/7 is the translational fraction. 1/2 and 2/5 do not match the calculation.","trap":"Translational 5/7, rotational 2/7 for a sphere.","answer":"2/7"},"phy-12-01-m24":{"idea":"For a ring rolling, the rotational and translational energies are equal.","steps":["I = MR² and ω = v/R.","Rotational KE = ½ MR² (v/R)² = ½ M v².","Translational KE = ½ M v².","Total = M v²."],"others":"(3/4)Mv² and (7/10)Mv² belong to other bodies. ½Mv² is only the translational part.","trap":"A ring has the most rotational energy for a given size.","answer":"Mv²"},"phy-12-01-m25":{"idea":"The body with the smallest k²/R² rolls down fastest.","steps":["Acceleration a = g sin θ/(1 + k²/R²).","k²/R²: solid sphere 2/5, disc 1/2, hollow sphere 2/3, ring 1.","The smallest value gives the biggest acceleration, so the solid sphere wins."],"others":"The hollow sphere and the ring have larger k²/R², so they are slower. The disc is slower than the solid sphere.","trap":"Mass and radius do not matter, only the shape.","answer":"solid sphere"},"phy-12-01-m26":{"idea":"Compare accelerations with a ∝ 1/(1 + k²/R²).","steps":["Solid cylinder: k²/R² = 1/2, so a ∝ 1/(1.5) = 2/3.","Hollow cylinder: k²/R² = 1, so a ∝ 1/2.","Ratio = (2/3)/(1/2) = 4/3."],"others":"2 : 1 and 1 : 1 do not match. 3 : 4 is the inverse.","trap":"Solid is faster, so the ratio is more than 1.","answer":"4 : 3"},"phy-12-01-m27":{"idea":"In uniform circular motion the net force points to the centre.","steps":["The speed is constant, but the direction keeps changing.","So there is an acceleration v²/r towards the centre.","The net force that causes it also points to the centre."],"others":"A force along the tangent would change the speed. A force away from the centre is centrifugal, which is not a real force in an inertial frame.","trap":"Centripetal means centre-seeking.","answer":"towards the centre of the circle"},"phy-12-01-m28":{"idea":"On a frictionless banked road the mass cancels out of the safe-speed equation.","steps":["Horizontal: N sin θ = m v²/r.","Vertical: N cos θ = m g.","Divide: tan θ = v²/(r g), so m cancels, and v = √(r g tan θ)."],"others":"The mass does not appear. So options that include the mass of the car are wrong.","trap":"The safe speed depends on r, θ and g only.","answer":"r, θ and g, but not on the mass of the car"},"phy-12-01-m29":{"idea":"To finish a vertical circle the string must stay taut at the top.","steps":["At the top, the least speed has v² = g r.","Energy conservation from bottom to top: ½v_b² = ½v_t² + 2 g r.","So v_b² = g r + 4 g r = 5 g r.","v_b = √(5 g r)."],"others":"√(3gr), √(2gr) and √(gr) are too small to keep the string taut at the top.","trap":"At the top: √(gr). At the bottom: √(5gr).","answer":"√(5gr)"},"phy-12-01-m30":{"idea":"A conical pendulum moves in a horizontal circle, with tension supplying both lift and centripetal force.","steps":["Vertical: T cos θ = m g.","Horizontal: T sin θ = m ω² (L sin θ).","So ω² = g/(L cos θ).","T_period = 2π/ω = 2π√(L cos θ/g)."],"others":"2π√(L/g) is the simple pendulum. With sin θ, the formula would be wrong. The last option has g and L the wrong way up.","trap":"cos θ appears because the circle's radius and tension both depend on θ.","answer":"2π√(L cosθ / g)"},"phy-12-01-n01":{"idea":"A thin ring about its centre axis has I = MR².","steps":["M = 2 kg and R = 0.5 m.","I = M R² = 2 × 0.25.","I = 0.50 kg m²."],"trap":"Square the radius before multiplying.","answer":"0.50 kg m²"},"phy-12-01-n02":{"idea":"First find I for a disc, then the rotational kinetic energy.","steps":["I = ½ M R² = ½ × 4 × 0.09 = 0.18 kg m².","KE = ½ I ω² = ½ × 0.18 × 20².","KE = ½ × 0.18 × 400 = 36.0 J."],"trap":"Square ω (20² = 400).","answer":"36.0 J"},"phy-12-01-n03":{"idea":"Torque gives angular acceleration, and then ω = αt from rest.","steps":["α = τ/I = 12/3 = 4 rad/s².","ω = α t = 4 × 5.","ω = 20.0 rad/s."],"trap":"Use τ = Iα first.","answer":"20.0 rad/s"},"phy-12-01-n04":{"idea":"Angular momentum is conserved when the skater pulls her arms in.","steps":["I₁ ω₁ = I₂ ω₂.","4 × 2 = 1.6 × ω₂.","ω₂ = 8/1.6 = 5.0 rad/s."],"trap":"A smaller I gives a larger ω.","answer":"5.0 rad/s"},"phy-12-01-n05":{"idea":"Use the parallel axis theorem to move from the centre to the end of a rod.","steps":["I_cm = ML²/12.","I = I_cm + M (L/2)² = ML²/12 + ML²/4 = ML²/3.","I = 3 × 1.44/3 = 1.44 kg m²."],"trap":"End of a rod: ML²/3.","answer":"1.44 kg m²"},"phy-12-01-n06":{"idea":"A rim axis is a distance R from the centre, so use the parallel axis theorem with d = R.","steps":["I_cm = ½ M R².","I = I_cm + M R² = (3/2) M R².","I = 1.5 × 2 × 0.04 = 0.12 kg m²."],"trap":"d is the distance between the two parallel axes.","answer":"0.12 kg m²"},"phy-12-01-n07":{"idea":"Rolling without slipping reduces the acceleration by the factor 1/(1 + k²/R²).","steps":["For a solid sphere k²/R² = 2/5.","a = g sin θ/(1 + 2/5) = (5/7) g sin θ.","a = (5/7) × 10 × 0.5 = 3.57 m/s²."],"trap":"A sliding block would have g sin θ = 5 m/s², more than this.","answer":"3.57 m/s²"},"phy-12-01-n08":{"idea":"Power is torque times angular velocity, in rad/s.","steps":["ω = 2π × 120/60 = 4π = 12.566 rad/s.","P = τ ω = 50 × 12.566.","P = 628.3 W."],"trap":"Change revolutions per minute to rad/s first.","answer":"628.3 W"},"phy-12-01-n09":{"idea":"On a banked road tan θ = v²/(r g).","steps":["v² = 400, r g = 40 × 10 = 400.","tan θ = 400/400 = 1.","θ = 45°."],"trap":"This only holds when there is no friction.","answer":"45°"},"phy-12-01-n10":{"idea":"Minimum speed at the bottom of a vertical circle is √(5 g r).","steps":["v² = 5 g r = 5 × 10 × 0.9 = 45.","v = √45 = 6.708.","v = 6.71 m/s."],"trap":"At the top the speed is √(gr) = 3 m/s.","answer":"6.71 m/s"}};
